{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Community-weighted mean fish body size (average biomass per individual) is lower in unprotected reefs than in fully protected reefs (‘area protegida’).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare mean individual fish size across protection categories.",  
      "steps":"1. From reef.csv, for each transect record compute individual size = biomass / quantity.  \n2. Aggregate to reef-year level: calculate community-weighted mean size by summing (individual size × quantity) over all records, then dividing by total quantity.  \n3. Label each reef by protection_status.  \n4. Fit a mixed-effects model: mean_size ~ protection_status + (1|id_reef).  \n5. If residuals violate normality, run a Kruskal–Wallis test on mean_size across protection_status.  \n6. Visualize results with boxplots of mean_size by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef-year mean sizes with protection status.  \n2. Mixed-effects model summary or Kruskal–Wallis test results.  \n3. Boxplot of mean fish size by protection status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Over the 2010–2020 period, protected reefs (‘area protegida’) show an increase in community-weighted mean fish size over time, while unprotected reefs do not.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for divergent temporal trends in mean fish size across protection levels.",  
      "steps":"1. As in Experiment 1, compute reef-year mean individual size.  \n2. Define a continuous time variable (year).  \n3. Fit a linear mixed-effects model: mean_size ~ year * protection_status + (1|id_reef).  \n4. Test the year×protection_status interaction for significance.  \n5. Plot fitted lines of mean_size vs. year for each protection_status (with confidence bands).",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mixed-effects model table with interaction term.  \n2. Line plot of mean fish size over years by protection status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Species evenness (Pielou’s J) is higher in fully protected reefs (‘area protegida’) than in unprotected reefs (‘sin proteccion’).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare species evenness across protection categories.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef-year, compute Shannon diversity: H = –∑ p_i log(p_i), where p_i = abundance_i / total abundance.  \n2. Compute Pielou’s evenness J = H / log(S), where S = species richness.  \n3. Merge J with protection_status.  \n4. Fit mixed-effects model: J ~ protection_status + (1|id_reef).  \n5. Alternatively, use Kruskal–Wallis on J across protection_status if assumptions fail.  \n6. Visualize with boxplots of J by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef-year evenness values and protection status.  \n2. Model or Kruskal–Wallis results.  \n3. Boxplot of Pielou’s J by protection status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The positive correlation between species richness and evenness is stronger in protected reefs (‘area protegida’) than in unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess differences in richness–evenness relationships among protection levels.",  
      "steps":"1. Use the reef-year richness and Pielou’s J from Experiment 3.  \n2. For each protection_status, compute Pearson (or Spearman) correlation between richness and J.  \n3. Fit a linear model: J ~ richness * protection_status.  \n4. Test the richness×protection_status interaction.  \n5. Plot scatterplots of J vs. richness with separate regression lines per protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Correlation coefficients by protection_status.  \n2. Model summary with interaction term.  \n3. Scatterplot of evenness vs. richness colored by protection_status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher baseline biomass (average 2010–2013) experience lower annual species turnover (Bray–Curtis) than reefs with low baseline biomass, indicating greater community stability.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether initial standing biomass predicts year-to-year community stability.",  
      "steps":"1. Aggregate reef-year total biomass.  \n2. Compute baseline biomass per reef = mean biomass from years 2010–2013.  \n3. From Experiment of turnover, compute annual turnover per reef-year pair.  \n4. Merge baseline biomass with turnover data.  \n5. Fit mixed-effects model: turnover ~ baseline_biomass + (1|id_reef).  \n6. Check residuals; if needed, run nonparametric correlation (Spearman) between baseline_biomass and turnover.  \n7. Plot turnover vs. baseline_biomass with fitted regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of reef turnover and baseline biomass.  \n2. Mixed-effects model or correlation results.  \n3. Scatterplot of turnover vs. baseline biomass."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Beta diversity (Bray–Curtis dissimilarity) between northern and southern regions has decreased from 2010 to 2020, indicating biogeographic homogenization.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for temporal decline in mean inter-regional dissimilarity over years.",  
      "steps":"1. Assign each id_reef to ‘north’ or ‘south’ region based on region field.  \n2. For each year, extract species-abundance matrices for all reefs in north and south.  \n3. Compute pairwise Bray–Curtis dissimilarities for all north-south reef pairs in each year; then compute mean inter-regional dissimilarity per year.  \n4. Fit a linear regression: mean_dissimilarity ~ year.  \n5. Test for a significant negative slope.  \n6. Plot mean inter-regional dissimilarity vs. year with regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Yearly mean inter-regional dissimilarity table.  \n2. Regression summary showing trend over time.  \n3. Line plot of mean dissimilarity vs. year."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Protected reefs (‘area protegida’) maintain a higher ratio of shallow-stratum biomass to deep-stratum biomass than unprotected reefs, reflecting better shallow zone health under protection.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare shallow:deep biomass ratios across protection levels.",  
      "steps":"1. Sum biomass for shallow stratum (e.g., 0–10 m) and deep stratum (e.g., 10–20 m) per reef-year.  \n2. Compute ratio = biomass_shallow / biomass_deep for each reef-year.  \n3. Merge ratio with protection_status.  \n4. Fit mixed-effects model: ratio ~ protection_status + (1|id_reef).  \n5. If assumptions fail, use Kruskal–Wallis on ratio across protection_status.  \n6. Visualize with boxplots of ratio by protection_status.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of shallow:deep ratio per reef-year with protection status.  \n2. Model or nonparametric test results.  \n3. Boxplot of shallow:deep biomass ratio by protection status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with higher baseline species richness (2010–2013 average) show smaller biomass declines during the 2014–2016 warming anomaly than reefs with lower baseline richness.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether initial diversity buffers against biomass loss in anomaly years.",  
      "steps":"1. Compute baseline richness per reef = mean species richness for years 2010–2013.  \n2. Compute mean biomass per reef in baseline and in anomaly period (2014–2016).  \n3. Calculate biomass change = biomass_anomaly − biomass_baseline for each reef.  \n4. Fit a linear regression: biomass_change ~ baseline_richness.  \n5. Test for a significant positive relationship (higher richness predicts less negative change).  \n6. Plot biomass_change vs. baseline_richness with fitted line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of baseline richness and biomass change per reef.  \n2. Regression summary for biomass change ~ baseline richness.  \n3. Scatterplot with regression line of biomass change vs. baseline richness."  
    }  
  }  
]}
